Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 15:47:11 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] rightkit-media: Windows recipe sets SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH so
 strip writes no timestamp

Two CI builds of ffprobe differed in four bytes: the PE TimeDateStamp that
FFmpeg's strip step writes, and the PE checksum that follows from it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 crates/rightkit-media/recipes/build-ffmpeg-windows.sh | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/crates/rightkit-media/recipes/build-ffmpeg-windows.sh b/crates/rightkit-media/recipes/build-ffmpeg-windows.sh
index e7b38af1..9f806973 100755
--- a/crates/rightkit-media/recipes/build-ffmpeg-windows.sh
+++ b/crates/rightkit-media/recipes/build-ffmpeg-windows.sh
@@ -209,8 +209,10 @@ EOF
   unset CFLAGS CXXFLAGS
 fi
 # Fully static: no libwinpthread-1.dll, libgcc or libstdc++ DLL. The PE timestamp is zeroed so
-# two builds of the same sources are comparable.
+# two builds of the same sources are comparable: --no-insert-timestamp covers the link, and
+# SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH covers FFmpeg's `strip` step, which otherwise stamps the current time.
 ffmpeg_flags+=(--extra-ldflags="-static -static-libgcc -Wl,--no-insert-timestamp")
+export SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=0
 
 src="$(unpack ffmpeg)"
 (cd "$src" && ./configure "${ffmpeg_flags[@]}" && make -j"$jobs" "$tool.exe")
